SpaceX, LunaNet, and the Governance of Interplanetary Communications

A Governance Analysis with Unified Control Primitive, Non-Compensatory Criteria, and Multilateral Enforcement Pathways

ouadi maakoul and SI TEAM

Abstract

The emergence of commercial lunar communications networks creates a governance question that extends beyond communications technology. NASA, ESA, JAXA, and other institutional actors are developing LunaNet as an interoperable, service-oriented, and extensible architecture intended to allow multiple governmental and commercial providers to operate within a common framework. Its networking architecture incorporates Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networking (DTN) and associated standardized interfaces. The LunaNet Interoperability Specification (LNIS) Version 5 was baselined and released on 29 January 2025; Version 6 "will be published soon" according to IOAG materials, though no public release has been confirmed as of October 2026.

At the same time, commercial providers are pursuing lunar communications capabilities through the extension of terrestrial constellation technologies. NASA selected SpaceX to provide laser communications for the Artemis III mission, installing two Starlink mini laser terminals—the same laser crosslink technology developed for the Starlink constellation—on the Orion spacecraft. SpaceX has publicly announced plans to extend Starlink connectivity to the Moon and eventually Mars through an interplanetary mesh.

However, SpaceX is not the only commercial or governmental actor pursuing lunar communications. ESA's Moonlight programme, JAXA's LNSS, NASA's LCRNS (contracted to Intuitive Machines), and China's Queqiao relay satellites represent parallel architectural trajectories. The governance question is therefore multilateral rather than bilateral.

This paper formalizes the governance problem around a single control primitive, proposes a revised Open-Infrastructure Criterion with non-compensatory aggregation and hard governance gates, analyzes enforcement pathways through procurement and licensing, maps the competitive landscape, and draws on terrestrial regulatory precedents. It concludes with specific, verifiable governance commitments that could be sought from any commercial provider of lunar communications infrastructure. The analysis identifies a transition-period enforcement gap: the verifiers required to operationalize the governance framework do not yet exist, and procurement conditions imposed by anchor customers constitute the only currently actionable governance mechanism. A specific, observable indicator of whether commercial providers will accept governance interoperability is whether they submit their convergence-layer specifications to CCSDS for standardization.

The paper's central structural claim—that protocol interoperability does not entail governance interoperability, and governance interoperability does not entail accountability—is conceptual: it follows from the separation of the three interoperability levels (Section 6) and of the accountability diagnostic from the structural governance score (Section 8.4). What the non-compensatory aggregation guarantees is that no score on protocol compliance or resilience can compensate for a failure of either hard gate (Section 9.3). This guarantee is a design property of the aggregation and gate rules and does not depend on particular parameter values, though the classification of borderline cases does.

