1. The Emerging Institutional Context

Lunar communications are transitioning from isolated mission-specific links toward a networked infrastructure model.

LunaNet is defined as "a set of cooperating networks providing interoperable communications and position, navigation, and timing (PNT) services for users in transit to, around, and on the Moon," based on "a framework of mutually agreed-upon standards, protocols, frequency bands, and interface requirements that enable interoperability." Its stated architectural principles include openness, service orientation, scalability, resilience, security, and extensibility.

The governance work is being coordinated through the Interagency Operations Advisory Group (IOAG). In 2023, the IOAG established the Committee to Study LunaNet Governance (CSLG) to "recommend an initial multi-stakeholder organizational governance structure, approach and functions to develop guidelines, policies and practices that enable the secure and efficient operational delivery of LunaNet services by multiple governmental and commercial providers." The IOAG is led by a Chairman appointed by member agency delegates, and its active working groups include the Lunar Communications and Navigation Working Group, the CSLG, and the Mars and Beyond Communication Architecture Working Group.

As of February 2026, the LunaNet Governance Report's chapters were in draft-complete status, with publication at the IOAG webpage targeted for April 2026. As of October 2026, no public confirmation of the report's release has been identified; its current status should be verified at the IOAG webpage. The emerging governance work is particularly important because technical interoperability alone does not determine who controls the infrastructure. A network can implement common protocols while remaining institutionally centralized.

