1.2 The Timing Asymmetry

A structural feature of the current situation deserves emphasis at the outset: the governance framework is being produced on a slower timescale than the commercial deployment it is meant to govern. LNIS Version 5 was baselined with many TBDs and undefined sections deferred to Version 6. The CSLG report chapters are draft-complete but unreleased. ARIN-2026-1 remains under discussion. Meanwhile, NASA has selected SpaceX to provide laser communications for Artemis III using Starlink mini laser terminals derived from more than 25,000 lasers already operating in low Earth orbit, and SpaceX has publicly announced plans to extend the same laser technology first to the Moon and then to Mars.

This timing asymmetry is the empirical context for the paper's central normative claim. The question is not whether governance is desirable in the abstract; it is whether governance mechanisms can be established while multiple providers are still entering the market, before any single provider's implementation becomes the de facto reference. The formal treatment of this claim appears in Section 17.

