3. LunaNet's Technical Architecture and the DTN Governance Gap

A central element of LunaNet is Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networking (DTN). DTN is the foundation of NASA's LunaNet architecture, enabling robust communications, navigation, and networking capabilities for long-term lunar presence.

DTN addresses intermittent connectivity through store-and-forward networking and the Bundle Protocol. Bundle Protocol version 7 (BPv7) is designed to handle intermittent connectivity, long delays, bit errors, and other common issues in a space-based network. DTN with Bundle Protocol has been selected as an interoperable network protocol in the LunaNet Interoperability Specification. The CCSDS Custody Transfer specification provides disruption tolerance by guaranteeing reliable delivery through bundle acknowledgements.

LNIS Version Status: LNIS Version 5 was baselined and released on 29 January 2025. Version 5 contains many TBDs and undefined sections planned to be resolved in LNIS Version 6, which is intended to complete the definition of LunaNet 1.0. The IOAG presentation of February 2026 states that "The LNIS Version 6 will be published soon." A NASA technical report confirms the update to the January 2025 release was "expected early 2026." Applicable documents under active development include AD5 (Lunar Reference Systems), AD6 (Data Services Encapsulation), and AD8 (LunaNet Interoperability Security Specification, which addresses authentication on AFS and P2P messages).

3.1 The Technical Objection: Is Store-and-Forward Right for High-Rate Optical Mesh?

A reviewer may reasonably raise a technical objection: DTN's store-and-forward architecture, designed for low-rate, intermittent links, may not be the appropriate networking layer for a high-bandwidth optical mesh providing continuous connectivity. If a commercial lunar architecture provides persistent laser links between orbital relays, the store-and-forward paradigm may introduce unnecessary latency and complexity.

This objection has merit and requires careful response. Three points are relevant:

First, DTN and high-rate links are not mutually exclusive. The Bundle Protocol operates above the convergence layer. A high-rate optical link can serve as a convergence layer beneath BPv7. The store-and-forward function becomes relevant only when links are disrupted. For a continuously connected mesh, bundles may traverse the network in near-real-time without intermediate storage.

Second, lunar topology includes inherently disruptive links. Surface assets on the far side, in permanently shadowed regions, or in line-of-sight-constrained locations cannot maintain continuous connectivity. The lunar communications architecture includes surface assets, orbital relays, and Earth ground stations with variable visibility. DTN provides the architectural framework for managing these disruptions within a common protocol suite.

Third, the governance question is independent of the technical layer. Even if a high-rate optical mesh uses a different networking layer for routine operations, the governance functions identified in this paper—node admission, authentication, routing, contact plan management, spectrum coordination—remain relevant. A proprietary optical mesh with closed interfaces raises the same governance concerns as a proprietary DTN network.

The appropriate response to the technical objection is therefore not to abandon DTN but to recognize that DTN is the standardized framework for the disruptive portions of the lunar network, while acknowledging that high-rate links may use optimized transport mechanisms beneath the DTN layer.

3.2 Contact Graph Routing and Custody Transfer

Two DTN functions have particular governance significance:

Contact Graph Routing (CGR). DTN networks require contact plans—schedules of when communication links between nodes will be available. The CCSDS has standardized Schedule-Aware Bundle Routing (SABR), the standardized form of CGR. The governance question is: who defines the contact plans? In a multi-provider network, contact plan management determines the topology and priority of the entire network. The CCSDS Lunar Interoperability Forum has identified "contact plan management automation" and "API for Intra and inter-LNSP network contact plan management" as active areas of development.

Custody Transfer. When a bundle is custody-transferred to a relay node, that node assumes responsibility for its delivery. In a multi-provider environment, custody transfer creates a chain of responsibility that crosses organizational boundaries. If a single provider operates the majority of relay nodes, it becomes a custodial chokepoint.

3.3 The Optical Convergence Layer as the Operative Question

The technical objection in Section 3.1 and the governance analysis in the rest of the paper intersect at a specific question: what is the convergence layer specification for high-rate optical links, and who controls it?

DTN's layered architecture separates the Bundle Protocol from the underlying link technology. BPv7 can run over any convergence layer that satisfies the relevant interface requirements. For radio-frequency links, the CCSDS has established standardized convergence layers (e.g., Proximity-1, AOS, TC/TM). For optical links, the situation is less settled. CCSDS has work underway on optical communications standards, but the maturity of these specifications relative to RF is lower, and the deployment of optical terminals at scale has occurred primarily in commercial constellations rather than in interoperable institutional architectures.

The Starlink laser terminals selected for Artemis III provide a concrete case. The public record establishes that the Orion spacecraft will carry two Starlink mini laser terminals, "based on the more than 25,000 lasers currently operating on-orbit." The public record does not establish whether the optical link layer follows a CCSDS optical convergence layer specification, nor whether the specification is published.

This matters for the governance analysis because the convergence layer is where the boundary between "open interface" and "proprietary implementation" is drawn in practice. If a commercial provider publishes its convergence layer specification and submits it to CCSDS for standardization, the interface becomes open and the implementation can remain proprietary—the "interface/implementation separation" principle. If the convergence layer specification remains internal to the provider, the interface itself is proprietary, and protocol-level compliance with BPv7 does not prevent the provider from controlling the physical layer on which all other participants depend.

A specific, observable indicator follows: whether a commercial provider submits its lunar optical convergence layer specification to CCSDS for standardization, and whether the submission is made with a commitment to non-discriminatory licensing of any essential patents. This indicator is more informative than the provider's stated intentions, because it is a costly and verifiable action rather than a public commitment.

