4. The Competitive Landscape

A governance analysis centered solely on one commercial provider would understate the multilateral character of the problem. The lunar communications landscape includes multiple actors pursuing parallel architectures.

4.1 ESA Moonlight

ESA's Moonlight programme aims to establish shared communications and navigation infrastructure supporting future EU and international lunar missions. The Moonlight LCNS (Lunar Communications and Navigation Service) is planned in two steps: Initial Operating Capability (IOC) in 2028 and Full Operating Capability (FOC) in 2030. The contractor is Telespazio. ESA is working closely with NASA and JAXA on the definition of joint lunar communication and navigation international standards, and Moonlight compliance to LunaNet standards will ensure technical compatibility with NASA Artemis missions.

The Moonlight model is instructive: ESA acts as an anchor customer, with a European service provider operating the constellation. This is a public-private partnership model that differs from both pure commercial provision and pure government operation. The consortium structure—Telespazio as prime, with Intuitive Machines as a strategic partner—introduces a multi-provider element at the European level that is absent in a single-provider architecture.

4.2 NASA LCRNS (Intuitive Machines)

NASA's Lunar Communications Relay and Navigation Systems (LCRNS) is contracted to Intuitive Machines. The first lunar relay launch is now projected for the first quarter of 2027. The reference constellation epoch is March 1, 2027. The IM-4 mission is expected to include the launch of two lunar data relay satellites. This represents a commercial service provision model in which NASA is the anchor customer but the infrastructure is privately operated.

Intuitive Machines' Lunar Data Network (LDN) is explicitly positioned "as part of LunaNet," and the company has stated that it will operate in compliance with LNIS. The company has also signed a strategic cooperation agreement with Telespazio covering "lunar communication and navigation services and systems," which creates a potential bridge between the NASA-contracted LCRNS and the ESA-contracted Moonlight architectures. This is an example of multi-provider participation emerging through commercial arrangements rather than through governance mandate.

4.3 JAXA LNSS

Japan's Lunar Navigation Satellite System (LNSS), contracted to ArkEdge Space, is planned for launch in 2028–2029. A 2029 LANS constellation concept includes two or three LCRNS satellites, one LCNS satellite, and one LNSS satellite. The first LNSS satellite is a demonstration mission, with the flight model completion targeted for December 2028. This adds a third governmental architecture to the landscape.

4.4 China Queqiao and ILRS

China's Queqiao relay satellites—Queqiao-1 deployed in 2018 and Queqiao-2 deployed in 2024—provide communications relay services for China's lunar exploration program and are positioned to support future international missions. The International Lunar Research Station (ILRS) represents a parallel governance framework to the Artemis Accords, organized around principles of "mutual consultation, joint construction, and shared benefits." The two frameworks coexist without mechanisms to share information or coordinate on communications architecture.

4.5 Implications for Governance

The existence of multiple competing architectures means that the governance question is not simply whether one commercial provider will comply with LunaNet. It is whether the emerging polycentric system can establish interoperability across architectures—LunaNet, Moonlight, ILRS, and commercial systems—or whether lunar communications will fragment into incompatible regional networks.

The risk of fragmentation is compounded by geopolitical competition. If the Artemis Accords and ILRS represent competing governance visions, lunar communications could become a vector of geopolitical division rather than cooperation. The governance objective should therefore include mechanisms for cross-architecture interoperability, not merely intra-LunaNet compliance. As noted in Section 2.4, this does not require institutional integration between LunaNet and ILRS; it requires that LunaNet specifications be designed so that future interoperability arrangements remain technically feasible. The DTN/Bundle Protocol foundation is general-purpose rather than LunaNet-specific, which is a useful property in this respect.

