6. Three Levels of Interoperability

The analysis requires distinguishing three levels, which we label consistently throughout the paper. The two non-implication statements that follow are expressed in the same vocabulary.

Level 1 — Protocol interoperability: Different systems can exchange information ($S_A \leftrightarrow S_B$).

Level 2 — Service interoperability: Users can obtain equivalent or complementary services from multiple providers ($U \rightarrow \{p_1, p_2, p_3, \ldots, p_n\}$).

Level 3 — Governance interoperability: No single provider possesses unilateral authority over network-wide shared coordination functions upon which all other providers depend:

\boxed{\neg\exists\,p\,\exists\,F \in \mathcal{F}_{\text{shared}}: \quad \text{ctrl}_F(p) > 1 - g_{\min}}

That is: there does not exist a provider $p$ and a shared function $F$ such that $p$'s control over $F$ exceeds the governance threshold. The restriction to $\mathcal{F}_{\text{shared}}$ is deliberate. Every provider necessarily exercises sole authority over its own relays and internal resources; that is not a governance failure. The concern arises only for functions that are shared across providers—such as root-of-trust operations, contact plan publication, or spectrum coordination—where unilateral control creates structural dependency.

This formulation is consistent with the gate condition $G \geq g_{\min}$: if $g_{\min} = 0.5$, then Level 3 requires $\text{ctrl}_F(p) \leq 0.5$ for all shared functions, which is a stricter and more meaningful condition than forbidding only $\text{ctrl}_F(p) = 1$.

Two separate non-implications should be stated. They are expressed in the vocabulary of Levels 1–3 and the accountability diagnostic $A$ (Section 8.4):

\boxed{\text{Protocol Interoperability (Level 1)} \;\not\Rightarrow\; \text{Governance Interoperability (Level 3)}}

\boxed{\text{Governance Interoperability (Level 3)} \;\not\Rightarrow\; \text{Accountability} \; (A)}

A commercial provider could satisfy Level 1 while failing Level 3, and could satisfy Level 3 while scoring low on the accountability diagnostic $A$. A network could expose an open protocol while retaining unilateral control over routing, spectrum access, authentication, node admission, and software updates. In that case, it is interoperable at the interface layer but still a proprietary chokepoint at the infrastructure layer. Conversely, a provider could pass the Level 3 structural test—because the player set is dispersed—while exercising its residual control in ways that are not transparent, not reviewable, and not auditable; that is a failure of accountability, not of structure, and the framework reports it separately as $A_F$.

6.1 The Threshold Is a Policy Choice, Not a Technical Constant

The Level 3 condition is parameterized by $g_{\min}$, and the operational meaning of the condition depends on the value chosen. If $g_{\min} = 0.5$, Level 3 requires that no provider exercise control above 0.5 on any shared function. This is a stringent condition. Applied to the current institutional landscape, it would likely classify every provider as failing Level 3, because in several shared functions—authentication and root of trust, contact plan management, endpoint identifier allocation—no provider currently exercises control below 0.5, and in most cases no governance body exists to constrain them.

This is not an argument against the formulation. It is an argument for treating $g_{\min}$ as a policy variable whose selection should be transparent and justified, and for recognizing that the transition from the current state to a state satisfying Level 3 is a governance project, not a classification exercise.

The threshold should be selected with reference to the following considerations:

· Legal precedent: Terrestrial telecommunications regulation typically imposes obligations on operators with significant market power, defined by market share thresholds (often 25–40%) combined with market analysis. The $g_{\min}$ threshold for lunar governance could be benchmarked against these precedents.
· Functional criticality: Functions whose failure would disable the network for all participants (root of trust, endpoint identifier registry) warrant lower $g_{\min}$ than functions whose failure would degrade performance without disabling service.
· Feasibility: A $g_{\min}$ that no provider can satisfy in the near term may be counterproductive if it discourages participation. A staged approach—higher $g_{\min}$ initially, tightening over time—may be more effective than a single stringent threshold.

The paper does not prescribe a specific value. It specifies the structural form of the condition and identifies the considerations that should govern the choice.

6.2 The Transition-Period Enforcement Gap

A second operational question follows from the Level 3 formulation: who determines $\text{ctrl}_F(p)$ in the transition period, before the governance bodies and verifiers described in Section 14.1 exist?

The commitment-to-variable mapping in Section 15.1 assigns each commitment to a verifier, but Section 14.1 acknowledges that these verifiers "do not currently exist." This is not a defect of the framework; it is a description of the current state. But it has an implication that should be stated explicitly: in the absence of established verifiers, the factual determination of $\text{ctrl}_F(p)$ will be made by whichever actor has the practical capacity to make it.

In the current landscape, that actor is most likely to be the anchor customer—NASA, ESA, or JAXA—acting through procurement conditions. The procurement mechanism is described in Section 11.1 as an enforcement pathway. In the transition period, it is more than that: it is the only currently actionable governance mechanism. The licensing pathway (Section 11.2) is jurisdictionally bounded and has not yet been applied to lunar communications governance. The WRC-27 pathway (Section 11.3) operates on a longer timescale. The Artemis Accords (Section 11.4) are normative rather than coercive.

This has three consequences for the analysis:

First, the procurement conditions imposed by anchor customers carry disproportionate weight in the transition period. If NASA requires LunaNet compatibility as a condition of the Artemis III laser communications contract, that condition is more consequential than any statement in an IOAG report. The public record does not establish whether such conditions were imposed for the Orion laser terminals, or if imposed, what their content was. Verifying this is a priority for further work.

Second, the transition-period determination of $\text{ctrl}_F(p)$ will be made on the basis of contractual leverage rather than neutral assessment. This is not necessarily illegitimate—anchor customers have legitimate interests in the interoperability of the infrastructure they fund—but it should be acknowledged. A procurement-based determination of governance compliance is not the same as a governance-body determination, and the difference matters for the legitimacy of the classification.

Third, the establishment of independent verifiers should be treated as a precondition for the full operation of the framework, not as a downstream implementation detail. The IOAG CSLG should prioritize the institutional design of the verifiers described in Section 14.1, including their funding, appointment, and independence requirements, over further refinement of the technical specifications. The specifications without verifiers are normative statements; the specifications with verifiers are governance.

