7. The Functions That Require Examination

The analysis focuses on seven shared functions. These constitute the set $\mathcal{F}_{\text{shared}}$ used throughout the formalization.

7.1 Node admission

Who determines which spacecraft or relay nodes may participate? A genuinely multi-provider architecture could define transparent technical and security requirements. A proprietary architecture could retain unilateral admission authority. The goal is not to remove the admission gate—security requires it—but to ensure that admission criteria are transparent, that denial is reviewable, and that break-glass revocation is subject to ex-post review. Export control and dual-use constraints on publishing interface specifications must also be addressed.

7.2 Authentication and identity

Who controls the trust architecture? If a single provider controls authentication credentials, certificates, or equivalent trust mechanisms, technical interoperability may still leave other actors dependent upon that provider. LNIS AD8 (LunaNet Interoperability Security Specification) addresses authentication on AFS and P2P messages, but the governance of the security architecture—who issues credentials, who revokes them, and under what conditions—remains undefined.

This is the function where the governance gap is most acute in the current landscape. AD8 is described as under active development, and its scope is authentication of message exchanges rather than governance of the root of trust. If a commercial provider operates its own PKI as the trust anchor for its lunar network, and that PKI is not federated with the trust architectures of other providers, then protocol-level compliance with BPv7 does not prevent the provider from controlling access to the network. The distinction between $G_F$ (structural control) and $A_F$ (observed accountability) in Section 8.4 is directly relevant: a provider could publish its credential issuance and revocation policies, submit to external audit, and still retain sole custody of the root of trust. The structural control would be unchanged. This is why accountability is reported as a diagnostic rather than folded into the governance score.

7.3 Routing and contact graph management

As discussed in Section 3.2, the governance of contact graph routing and custody transfer policies is a deeper form of routing control. If a single provider controls the contact plans that determine when and how data is forwarded across the network, it effectively controls the network's topology and priorities—even if the underlying protocols are open.

7.4 Node identifiers and address allocation

In DTN, node identity runs through ipn or dtn endpoint identifiers and their allocation registry. The ipn URI scheme was originally defined in RFC 6260 for the experimental Bundle Protocol version 6 and subsequently updated for BPv7 by RFC 9758. The CBHE node-number registry and the IANA delegation to the Space Assigned Numbers Authority (SANA) for CCSDS missions are specified in RFC 7116. IANA has allocated the range 16384–2097151 (0x4000–0x1FFFFF) to SANA for use by CCSDS missions. This is a stronger chokepoint candidate than IP addressing, because DTN need not use IP at all. Whoever controls the endpoint identifier registry controls a shared namespace on which all DTN participants depend.

RFC 9758 (May 2025, Proposed Standard) updates RFC 6260, RFC 7116, and RFC 9171, and introduces a hierarchical structure for ipn URIs in which an Allocator Identifier identifies the entity responsible for allocating node numbers within a subspace. The default allocator is SANA. An organization can apply to hold its own Allocator Identifier, which delegates a node-number subspace to that organization for its own administration. This introduces a second, more granular observable proxy for control of the DTN namespace: whether a provider's lunar nodes are assigned under the default SANA allocator—with SANA recording each allocation—or under the provider's own Allocator Identifier.

Holding a delegated Allocator Identifier is not in itself a governance failure. It can be an efficient arrangement when the provider operates a large constellation and needs to manage node numbers at scale. The governance question is whether the delegation terms are public, whether the delegated space is revocable, and whether the provider publishes its allocation records within its subspace. A provider that holds an Allocator Identifier and publishes its allocation records has a transparent, auditable namespace. A provider that holds an Allocator Identifier and treats its subspace as internal operational data has effectively removed a portion of the shared namespace from public coordination. The observable indicator in either case is the provider's publication practice, not the existence of the delegation.

In parallel, the ARIN Draft Policy ARIN-2026-1, "Taking IP To Other Planets" (TIPTOP), addresses IP address allocation for space networks. The policy was accepted as a Draft Policy on 19 March 2026 and revised on 27 May 2026. The policy proposes that addressing structures "should be organized hierarchically to reflect major celestial regions—such as the Moon, Earth–Moon Lagrange points, and other planetary systems—enabling route aggregation where feasible." ARIN-2026-1 remained under discussion as of the latest available information. The governance question is: who coordinates address allocation across multiple RIRs and space agencies?

The DTN endpoint identifier allocation is the more consequential of the two, because a DTN network does not require IP at all. If a commercial provider's lunar network uses proprietary endpoint identifiers rather than SANA-registered ipn numbers, the network can be BPv7-compliant while remaining outside the shared namespace. If the network uses SANA-registered numbers under the provider's own Allocator Identifier but the allocation decisions within that subspace are not published, the shared namespace is formally respected but practically controlled. The verifiable indicator is whether the provider publishes its endpoint identifier allocation records—whether under the default SANA allocator or under its own Allocator Identifier—and participates in SANA coordination.

7.5 Spectrum coordination

Spectrum is inherently a shared resource. One of the central LunaNet tenets is the use of shared spectrum. The Lunar Augmented Navigation Service requires that LunaNet Service Providers use the same frequency band (2483.5 MHz–2500.0 MHz) and transmit the same waveform synchronized by highly accurate clocks so that users "see" one virtual network. JAXA, ESA, and NASA comply with the LNIS to become interoperable LunaNet Service Providers.

The governance question is therefore not simply whether a provider can operate its own frequencies. It is: how are conflicts among independent users resolved? This coordination is being addressed through the Space Frequency Coordination Group, and the 2483.5–2500 MHz band is included in WRC-27 Agenda Item 1.15 for in-situ PNT.

7.6 Protocol evolution

Suppose multiple lunar operators depend on a common protocol. Who decides when that protocol changes? If $p_1, p_2, p_3, \ldots, p_n$ depend upon a common networking interface, unilateral modification by one provider could impose costs upon every other participant. Protocol evolution therefore becomes a governance function.

The LNIS Version 6 delay illustrates the tension. Version 5 contains many TBDs and undefined sections; Version 6 is intended to resolve them and complete the definition of LunaNet 1.0. The IOAG's February 2026 statement that Version 6 "will be published soon" was not accompanied by a confirmed release as of October 2026. If a commercial provider deploys a lunar network before Version 6 is finalized, its implementation becomes a reference point for the eventual standard, and the standard-setting process is constrained by the existing deployment. This is not necessarily improper—deployments often inform standards—but it means that the protocol evolution function is being exercised de facto by whichever provider deploys first, rather than de jure by the standards body.

7.7 Traffic management

As lunar activity increases, communications resources could become contested. Questions could eventually include priority during emergencies, mission-critical traffic, scientific traffic, commercial traffic, governmental traffic, and humanitarian or safety communications.

This function is not yet contested, because lunar traffic volumes remain low. But it is the function where the governance gap is most likely to become acute as activity increases. If a single provider operates the majority of relay nodes, and if that provider also operates the traffic management function, then priority rules—including rules that affect safety-critical and scientific traffic—would be determined unilaterally. The governance objective is not to specify the priority rules in advance, but to ensure that the rules are made through a process in which all affected parties have a voice.

