8. The Chokepoint Problem: A Unified Control Primitive

8.1 The Control Primitive

The central risk can be formalized around a single primitive: the degree of unilateral control that a provider exercises over a shared function. For each shared function $F \in \mathcal{F}_{\text{shared}}$ and provider $p$, define:

\boxed{\text{ctrl}_F(p) \in [0, 1]}

This primitive combines two components:

· Technical control: custody of root keys, control of the endpoint identifier registry, publication of contact plans, control of protocol implementations;
· Formal voting power: the Banzhaf power index of $p$ in any governance body with decision rights over $F$, normalized to $[0, 1]$.

The functional form is a weighted combination:

\boxed{\text{ctrl}_F(p) = \lambda \cdot \text{tech}_F(p) + (1 - \lambda) \cdot \text{BPI}_F(p)}

Where $\lambda \in [0, 1]$ is a policy parameter reflecting the relative weight of technical control versus formal voting power. The Banzhaf index requires a defined player set (agencies, states, providers) and a quota rule; these must be specified by the governance body before $G$ can be computed. When no governance body exists and a single provider exercises sole control, $\text{ctrl}_F(p) = 1$ and the per-function governance score $G_F = 0$ by convention. This convention addresses the $n = 1$ singularity.

8.2 Dependency Measure

The dependency measure weights each shared function by its criticality and replaceability:

\boxed{D_F(p) = c_F \cdot (1 - r_F) \cdot \text{ctrl}_F(p)}

\boxed{D(p) = \max_{F \in \mathcal{F}_{\text{shared}}} D_F(p)}

Where:

· $c_F \in [0, 1]$ is the criticality weight of function $F$;
· $r_F \in [0, 1]$ is the replaceability of function $F$;
· $\text{ctrl}_F(p) \in [0, 1]$ is the degree of unilateral control.

The vector $\{D_F(p)\}_{F \in \mathcal{F}_{\text{shared}}}$ should be reported alongside the maximum. The maximum is used primarily as the gate variable; the vector provides diagnostic information.

8.3 Governance Score

The per-function governance score is the complement of normalized control:

\boxed{G_F(p) = 1 - \text{ctrl}_F(p)}

The overall governance variable is the minimum across shared functions:

\boxed{G(p) = \min_{F \in \mathcal{F}_{\text{shared}}} G_F(p)}

The minimum reflects the fact that a single chokepoint function—such as sole control of the root of trust—can dominate the governance assessment even if other functions are well-governed.

8.4 Accountability as a Separate Diagnostic

$G$ measures how much control a provider exercises. The commitments in Section 15 constrain how that control is used. These are distinct: a sole controller with published admission criteria and reviewable revocation still has $\text{ctrl}_F = 1$. Rather than folding accountability into $G$—which would make the governance score compensatory and would allow a sole controller with perfect transparency to pass the gate—the framework reports a separate accountability score:

\boxed{A_F(p) \in [0, 1]}

based on observed behavior: published criteria, reviewable denials, ex-post revocation review, published contact plan APIs, and published interconnection terms. The structural governance score $G_F = 1 - \text{ctrl}_F(p)$ continues to feed Level 3, the gate condition, and the $O_I$ aggregation. $A_F$ is reported alongside $G_F$ as a diagnostic and may in future work be added as a fourth factor in $O_I$ if a governance body chooses to weight observed accountability separately. The default framework keeps them separate to preserve the non-compensatory property of the gate.

8.5 Interpretation

As $D(p) \rightarrow 1$, the systemic importance of the provider increases. As $G(p) \rightarrow 0$, the governance structure fails to constrain unilateral control over at least one shared function. This does not prove monopolistic behavior. It identifies infrastructure concentration. The objective should not be to prohibit commercial concentration automatically, but to ensure that critical dependencies remain governable, replaceable, and interoperable.

8.6 Robustness Against Threshold Gaming

The weights $c_F$ and $r_F$ are policy parameters. A provider could attempt to lower $D$ cosmetically by splitting functions or claiming nominal replaceability. Three safeguards apply:

· Audited indicators: $c_F$, $r_F$, and $\text{ctrl}_F$ should be set by an independent body with audited evidence, not self-reported.
· Hysteresis: designations should require crossing a threshold by a margin, and should not be reversed by marginal changes, following the EU significant market power (SMP) model.
· Due process: designation should follow a public process with notice, comment, and appeal.

Margin around the gates. The hysteresis safeguard requires an explicit rule for the gate thresholds. For the governance gate, let $\delta_G > 0$ be the hysteresis margin. A network that is currently classified as failing the gate ($G_{\text{network}} < g_{\min}$) is reclassified as passing only if $G_{\text{network}} \geq g_{\min} + \delta_G$; a network currently classified as passing is reclassified as failing only if $G_{\text{network}} < g_{\min} - \delta_G$. Within the band $[g_{\min} - \delta_G, g_{\min} + \delta_G]$, the current classification is preserved. The same construction applies to the dependency gate with margin $\delta_D$ around $d_{\max}$, with the inequalities reversed because lower $D$ is better: a currently failing network ($D_{\text{network}} > d_{\max}$) is reclassified as passing only if $D_{\text{network}} \leq d_{\max} - \delta_D$, and a currently passing network is reclassified as failing only if $D_{\text{network}} > d_{\max} + \delta_D$. Without this rule, a provider could oscillate around the threshold with marginal changes to $c_F$ or $r_F$ and force repeated reclassification. The margins $\delta_G$ and $\delta_D$ are policy parameters and should be published alongside $g_{\min}$ and $d_{\max}$; a natural starting point is $\delta_G = \delta_D = 0.02$, but the choice should be justified by the governance body.

8.7 The Transition-Period Measurement Problem

The safeguards above presuppose the existence of an independent body. In the transition period, no such body exists. The practical consequence is that $\text{ctrl}_F(p)$ will be assessed either by the provider itself, by an anchor customer through contractual mechanisms, or by external analysts on the basis of public information.

The framework should be explicit about this. The following measurement protocol is proposed for the transition period:

· Self-assessment with disclosure: providers publish their own assessment of $\text{ctrl}_F(p)$ for each shared function, with supporting evidence, on a public register.
· Anchor-customer verification: where the provider operates under contract with an anchor customer (NASA, ESA, JAXA, or another agency), the customer verifies the self-assessment to the extent permitted by contract and publishes the verification.
· Independent review: academic and civil-society analysts review the self-assessments and verifications, and publish their own assessments. This is not a substitute for institutional verification, but it provides a check on self-reporting.
· Provisional classification: classifications made under the transition-period protocol are explicitly provisional and are subject to revision when independent verifiers are established.

This protocol is not ideal. It is, however, more useful than waiting for the governance bodies to be established before any assessment is made, because by the time the bodies exist, the dependency may already have formed.

