9. Formalization of the Open-Infrastructure Criterion

9.1 The Compensatory Problem

A linear weighted sum is compensatory: high scores on some variables can mask low scores on others. This contradicts the paper's central thesis. A network scoring 1 on protocol compliance and resilience but 0 on governance would still receive a high score under linear aggregation, which is precisely the "interoperable at the interface, controlled at the infrastructure" case the paper argues against.

9.2 Revised Non-Compensatory Aggregation

The Open-Infrastructure Criterion uses a weighted geometric mean over three variables:

\boxed{O_I = I^{w_I} \cdot G^{w_G} \cdot E^{w_E}, \quad w_I + w_G + w_E = 1, \quad w_k > 0}

Where:

· $I \in [0, 1]$ is protocol compliance;
· $G \in [0, 1]$ is governance interoperability, as defined in Section 8.3;
· $E \in [0, 1]$ is resilience, defined as the fraction of critical services preserved when the largest provider is removed (Section 14.2).

Provider plurality is not included in $O_I$. Market concentration is reported as context (Section 9.4) but does not enter the aggregation, because a concentrated market can be well-governed and a dispersed market can be poorly governed. The relevant question is control over shared functions, which is captured by $G$ and $D$.

Under a geometric mean, if any variable is zero, $O_I = 0$. A network that fails governance interoperability cannot compensate with high protocol compliance. It must be acknowledged that the geometric mean is only weakly non-compensatory: with $w_G = 0.1$ and $G = 0.1$ (and $I = E = 1$), $O_I = 0.1^{0.1} \approx 0.79$, which would fall in the "open" range if the gates were absent. The hard gates in Section 9.3 therefore do the substantive work of preventing compensation.

9.3 Hard Governance Gates

In addition to the geometric aggregation, the following hard gates are imposed:

\boxed{G \geq g_{\min} \quad \text{and} \quad D(p) \leq d_{\max}}

Where $g_{\min}$ is a minimum governance threshold and $d_{\max}$ is a maximum acceptable chokepoint dependency. Failing either gate results in classification as a closed platform, regardless of other scores.

Unit clarification. $G(p)$ and $D(p)$ are defined per provider, whereas $O_I$ and $E$ are network-level quantities. For the network-level assessment, the gate variables are defined as the worst case across all providers in the network:

G_{\text{network}} = \min_{p \in \mathcal{P}} G(p), \qquad D_{\text{network}} = \max_{p \in \mathcal{P}} D(p)

where $\mathcal{P}$ is the set of providers operating in the network. The gates are applied to $G_{\text{network}}$ and $D_{\text{network}}$. This is the natural choice: a network is only as governable as its least-governable provider, and its chokepoint risk is determined by its most-controlling provider. A network with one provider that fails the gate is classified as a closed platform, even if other providers in the network pass. The worked example in Section 9.7 instantiates this by scoring archetypes, each of which represents a network with a single dominant provider.

9.4 Contextual Reporting: Market Concentration

The Herfindahl–Hirschman Index, $\text{HHI} = \sum_i s_i^2$, is reported as context. Markets with an HHI greater than 1,800 (on the 0–10,000 scale) are conventionally considered highly concentrated. The HHI is not used in $O_I$ because market share is neither necessary nor sufficient for chokepoint control. A provider with 90% market share but replaceable functions is not a chokepoint; a provider with 10% market share but sole custody of the root of trust is.

9.5 Variable Definitions

