9.8 Sensitivity Analysis

The weights $w_I$, $w_G$, $w_E$ and the thresholds $g_{\min}$, $d_{\max}$ are policy parameters. A full sensitivity analysis samples weights from a Dirichlet distribution over the simplex and sweeps $g_{\min} \in [0.3, 0.7]$ and $d_{\max} \in [0.3, 0.7]$, reporting the fraction of the parameter space in which each archetype receives each classification. The paper's structural claim is that the hard gates prevent compensation: a network that fails either gate is classified as a closed platform regardless of its $I$ and $E$ scores. This claim is a design property of the aggregation and gate rules, and holds for all parameter values in the stated ranges; it does not depend on the outcome of the sensitivity analysis. What the sensitivity analysis would establish is the robustness of the classifications of borderline archetypes. That analysis should be conducted by the IOAG CSLG or an independent research group and published alongside the framework. The paper does not claim to have demonstrated it.

9.9 Threshold Classification

· Open infrastructure: $O_I \geq 0.8$ AND $G_{\text{network}} \geq g_{\min}$ AND $D_{\text{network}} \leq d_{\max}$
· Gated / conditional platform: $0.5 \leq O_I < 0.8$ AND $G_{\text{network}} \geq g_{\min}$ AND $D_{\text{network}} \leq d_{\max}$
· Closed platform: $O_I < 0.5$ OR $G_{\text{network}} < g_{\min}$ OR $D_{\text{network}} > d_{\max}$

A network that fails either gate is classified as closed, regardless of its $O_I$. There is no separate category for "fails gate narrowly." The gate is binary, and the hysteresis margins described in Section 8.6 apply to the gate variables, not to the classification itself.

9.10 Regulatory Trigger: The Significant-Market-Power Analogy

The terrestrial unbundled local loop (ULL) regime was imposed on incumbents with significant market power over essential facilities. An analogous trigger for lunar communications would apply governance obligations only when $D(p)$ exceeds a threshold—following the significant-market-power logic, which involves market definition, market analysis, and public consultation before obligations are imposed. This answers the objection that the framework penalizes success by targeting only providers whose control of critical functions creates structural dependency.

