11. Enforcement and Leverage Analysis

The governance question is not merely normative but instrumental: who can compel what?

11.1 NASA Procurement Clauses

NASA is an anchor customer for lunar communications services. The Artemis III laser communications selection demonstrates NASA's willingness to adopt commercial laser terminals for institutional missions. However, the selection uses standard Starlink mini lasers, and the public record does not establish whether the Orion link follows CCSDS optical standards. A sharper test of openness would be conformance to CCSDS optical specifications—or, as discussed in Section 3.3, submission of the convergence layer specification to CCSDS for standardization.

NASA's leverage is also reciprocal. NASA depends on SpaceX for Human Landing System capabilities, and Blue Origin's second HLS award tempers the mutual-dependency relationship further. A more careful bargaining-power analysis would consider the alternatives available to each party and the cost of delay to each. The general point remains: procurement conditions are a direct and enforceable mechanism, but their effectiveness depends on the structure of the relationship.

Leverage: High but reciprocal. NASA procurement is a direct mechanism, constrained by mutual dependency. In the transition period, it is the most consequential governance mechanism available.

11.2 Licensing Conditions (FCC/NTIA)

National licensing authorities can impose interoperability and governance requirements as conditions of spectrum and launch licenses. The FCC has authority over commercial satellite operations, and NTIA coordinates federal spectrum use.

Leverage: High, but jurisdictionally bounded. Divergent licensing regimes create regulatory arbitrage risk.

11.3 WRC-27 Spectrum Conditions

WRC-27 Agenda Item 1.15 will establish spectrum allocations for lunar communications. Spectrum conditions could include interoperability requirements, shared-spectrum obligations, and coordination procedures that function as governance mechanisms.

Leverage: Medium. ITU decisions are binding on administrations but enforcement depends on national implementation.

11.4 Artemis Accords Interoperability Provisions

The Artemis Accords include provisions on interoperability in Section 5, committing signatories to "use reasonable efforts to utilize current interoperability standards for space-based infrastructure" and to establish standards when they do not exist. As of September 2026, 76 States had signed. These are principles rather than binding commitments. The Accords' effectiveness depends on signatory states incorporating them into national licensing regimes.

Leverage: Low to medium. Normative rather than coercive.

11.5 The Priority of Enforcement Pathways in the Transition Period

The four pathways above differ substantially in their current operational availability:

Pathway Current Status Time to Effect Jurisdictional Reach
NASA/ESA/JAXA procurement Operational Immediate Contractual
FCC/NTIA licensing Operational Months United States
WRC-27 spectrum conditions In preparation 2027–2030 Near-universal
Artemis Accords Signed Indeterminate 76 signatories

The procurement pathway is the only one that can operate immediately and that can impose governance conditions on a provider operating under an existing or imminent contract. It is therefore the pathway on which the transition-period governance strategy should focus. The licensing pathway will become more consequential as lunar communications services enter routine operation, and the WRC-27 pathway will establish the spectrum framework within which all other pathways operate. But in the period in which the governance framework is being established and the first commercial lunar networks are being deployed, procurement conditions imposed by anchor customers are the operative mechanism.

This has a specific implication for institutional design. If the procurement pathway is the operative mechanism in the transition period, then the governance framework should be designed to be implementable through procurement conditions. The commitments in Section 15 are formulated with this in mind: they are specific, verifiable, and capable of being incorporated into a statement of work or a contract clause. The commitment-to-variable mapping in Section 15.1 identifies the evidence and verifier for each commitment, which is the information an anchor customer would need to impose and monitor the condition.

