12. Terrestrial Regulatory Precedents

12.1 Subsea Fiber Optic Cable Consortiums (EASSy)

The Eastern African Submarine Cable System (EASSy) is a submarine fiber-optic cable developed by a consortium of more than 20 telecommunications operators with support from development finance institutions. It is based on an open-access model of governance that ensures "access to the cable is available to all operators within the region, irrespective of whether they are part of the consortium."

Mismatch caveat: EASSy was DFI-backed consortium financing, a very different power structure from a self-financed vertical integrator. The precedent is instructive but not directly analogous.

12.2 Unbundled Local Loop

The unbundled local loop (ULL) regulatory framework separates physical-layer infrastructure operation from service-layer provision. The FCC's Line Sharing Order was released on 9 December 1999, requiring incumbent local exchange carriers to provide competitive local exchange carriers access to the high-frequency portion of the local loop. The European Parliament and Council enacted Regulation No 2887/2000 on 18 December 2000, applicable to operators with significant market power.

Mismatch caveat: ULL was imposed on incumbents with significant market power over essential facilities. This gives a natural trigger: obligations apply only when $D(p)$ crosses a threshold.

12.3 ITU Open Access Rules for Satellite Slots

ITU regulations manage a cooperative satellite frequency registration process. The 1988 World Administrative Radio Conference (WARC-ORB-88) established an allotment plan that provided each ITU Member country with one orbital position and associated frequencies for one national satellite.

Mismatch caveat: ITU practice is largely first-come-first-served, so "shared international resource" overstates the cooperative dimension.

12.4 Intelsat/Inmarsat Privatization and Oversight Organizations

The privatization of Intelsat and Inmarsat created a model in which public-service obligations are overseen by intergovernmental organizations. The International Telecommunications Satellite Organization (ITSO) oversees Intelsat's public telecommunications obligations, and the International Mobile Satellite Organization (IMSO) oversees Inmarsat's Global Maritime Distress and Safety System (GMDSS) obligations.

The ITSO Core Principles include non-discriminatory access and fair and equal opportunity to access the privatized Intelsat system. The obligations therefore survived privatization, but enforcement leverage was weak. The lesson is not that obligations disappear, but that intergovernmental oversight without strong enforcement tools has limited effect.

12.5 IETF/ICANN/IANA for Protocol Evolution and Root of Trust

The Internet's governance model—IETF for protocol development, ICANN for policy coordination, and the IANA functions for root-of-trust operations—provides a precedent for neutral, multi-stakeholder governance of critical shared functions. The IANA functions (IP address allocation, domain name system root zone management, protocol parameter registries) are performed by Public Technical Identifiers (PTI), an ICANN affiliate, under contract with ICANN and subject to community oversight. The US stewardship transition completed in 2016.

Relevance and caveat: The IANA model demonstrates that root-of-trust functions can be operated by a neutral entity under multi-stakeholder oversight, rather than by a single provider. It does not by itself identify who the lunar equivalent would be; that is a design question for the IOAG CSLG and its successors. The SANA registry, which administers the DTN endpoint identifier space under the default Allocator Identifier, is a partial instantiation of this model in the space domain, but its governance relationship to the IOAG CSLG is not formalized.

12.6 FRAND/RAND Commitments in Standards Bodies

Standards bodies such as ETSI, IEEE, and ITU require participants to commit to licensing essential patents on fair, reasonable, and non-discriminatory (FRAND) terms. ETSI defines an IPR Policy based on FRAND licensing commitment, balancing standardization for public use with the rights of IPR owners.

Relevance: Direct. The FRAND model provides a template for "open interface, closed implementation," and applies specifically to the convergence layer specification discussed in Section 3.3.

12.7 Cospas-Sarsat

Cospas-Sarsat is an international satellite system for search and rescue, established in 1979 and now involving multiple countries and organizations. It provides a precedent for international cooperation on shared space infrastructure with public-service obligations.

Relevance and caveat: Cospas-Sarsat's operators are state agencies, so it weakly informs commercial dependency. It demonstrates that a shared, safety-critical space service can be governed through multilateral cooperation, but it does not show how a commercial provider would be bound.

