15. The Question Posed to Commercial Providers

Against this background, the following question is proposed. It is addressed to any commercial provider of lunar communications infrastructure, and it intentionally does not presuppose malicious intent. It asks providers to distinguish proprietary implementation from exclusive control of shared infrastructure functions.

Given the emerging LunaNet governance framework, the polycentric institutional landscape, and the Open, Service-Oriented, and Extensible principles of LunaNet—and given that DTN/Bundle Protocol is the defined networking foundation for LunaNet interoperability—will commercial providers commit to operating their planned cislunar communications systems as LunaNet-compatible service providers, including:

(a) Technical commitments: Support for standardized DTN interfaces, BPv7 and CCSDS Custody Transfer compliance, submission of the optical convergence layer specification to CCSDS for standardization with FRAND licensing of essential patents, interoperable identity mechanisms, and support for authenticated external nodes;

(b) Governance commitments: Participation in the IOAG CSLG multi-stakeholder governance structure, transparent and published procedures for node admission, defined mechanisms for protocol evolution subject to multi-provider consensus, participation in coordinated spectrum management through the SFCG and ITU processes, and published contact plan management and custody transfer policies;

(c) Resilience commitments: Agreement to independent audit of network resilience using the metrics proposed in Section 14.2, and support for multi-homing, alternative routing, and identity federation across providers;

(d) Address coordination: Participation in coordinated DTN endpoint identifier allocation through SANA/IANA—including, where the provider holds a delegated Allocator Identifier under RFC 9758, publication of the allocation records within its subspace and acceptance of revocability terms—and in the ARIN TIPTOP framework and equivalent regional internet registry processes for hierarchical address allocation organized by celestial region.

