16. Why This Matters Beyond the Moon

If humanity develops permanent settlements beyond Earth, communications infrastructure becomes part of civilization's basic infrastructure. The architecture could evolve toward:

\text{Earth} \leftrightarrow \text{LEO} \leftrightarrow \text{Cislunar} \leftrightarrow \text{Moon} \leftrightarrow \text{Mars} \leftrightarrow \text{Other destinations}

The lunar CPNT architecture is explicitly envisioned to be "the basis—with adjustments—of the future Mars CPNT architecture as we expand into the solar system using Interplanetary Networking (IPN)."

Governance cascade: The governance decisions made now for lunar communications will directly shape Mars governance architecture. If LunaNet's multi-stakeholder governance structure establishes principles of open interfaces, transparent node admission, and shared spectrum coordination for the Moon, those principles become the default template for extending the network to Mars. Conversely, if a single commercial provider establishes proprietary control over critical lunar communications functions, those control patterns will likely replicate at Mars, where the technical and political barriers to restructuring governance are substantially higher.

The convergence layer question discussed in Section 3.3 is particularly consequential for the Mars cascade. If the lunar optical convergence layer specification is standardized through CCSDS, the same specification can be extended to Mars links with modifications for distance and topology. If the specification remains proprietary at the lunar stage, the Mars extension inherits the proprietary constraint. The governance decision made at the lunar stage determines the default at the Mars stage.

The address allocation question discussed in Section 7.4 is similarly consequential. The hierarchical Allocator Identifier structure introduced by RFC 9758 is scalable to interplanetary distances and multiple celestial regions. A provider that holds a delegated Allocator Identifier at the lunar stage and publishes its allocation records establishes a practice that can be extended to Mars. A provider that treats its Allocator Identifier subspace as internal operational data establishes the opposite practice. The governance decision made at the lunar stage again determines the default at the Mars stage.

